WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.799
Last time we talked about Bohmian mechanics,
a possible alternative to Quantum mechanics.

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:10.750
One of the appeals of Bohmian mechanics is
that we can always, at any time, say this

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:13.370
is the position of this particle.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:18.021
On the other hand, with Quantum mechanics,
the superposition principle forces to say

00:00:18.021 --> 00:00:23.070
things like, the particle is in many different
states at the same time.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:28.030
With Bohmian mechanics, it's as though reality
properly exists regardless of whether we've

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.800
measuring or not.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:30.940
Or does it?

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.430
Today we're going to look at a potentially
big problem with Bohmian mechanics.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.250
The issue is something called Contextuality.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.640
Let me explain it

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:44.600
With any sensible theory you want measurement
to reveal a property that a particle already

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.460
had, not create or change it.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:54.129
I should point out, under this criterion,
Quantum mechanics is clearly not sensible.

00:00:54.129 --> 00:00:58.379
But from what we was last time, it seems like
Bohmian mechanics is.

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:03.050
So if you measure, say the spin of a particle
in the y direction, and you find it to be

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:08.560
spin up, in Bohmian mechanics, you want to
be able to say, then that particle had spin

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.060
up before hand.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.950
But what about the case where there is more
than one way to measure the spin of a particle?

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:20.700
Another sensible sensibility condition is
that different ways to measure something should

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.150
agree, so both those measurements should give
the same answer, right?

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:30.049
We'll call this noncontextuality- because
the result of the measurement shouldn't depend

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:32.869
on the experimental context.

00:01:32.869 --> 00:01:35.080
This is where the problem is though.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.040
In Bohmian mechanics this doesn't always happen.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.260
So instead of being always able to say statements
like "this particle has spin up in the y direction"

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:48.799
you are instead are reduced to saying things
like, "this particle has spin up in the y

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:54.670
direction if you measure it this way, but
spin down if you measure it another way".

00:01:54.670 --> 00:02:00.070
It's still perfectly deterministic, there
is no randomness but... it's just not that

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:01.260
nice.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.950
This is what we call contextuality.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.600
Let me show you an example of this happening.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.599
This example is going to be about the spin
of an electron.

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:14.680
Don't panic if you've forgotten all about
spin, I'm only going to use some very basic

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.340
facts about it.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:21.800
If you want a refresher on spin though, you
can go watch this video about it.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.519
First remember that every electron has it's
spin pointing in one particular direction.

00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:33.249
However, it's also possible to measure the
spin of the electron in any direction and

00:02:33.249 --> 00:02:35.819
this is where it gets weird.

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:40.079
Whatever direction you pick, there are only
two possible values you can get, which we

00:02:40.079 --> 00:02:45.309
call spin up and spin down for reason that
you'll see soon.

00:02:45.309 --> 00:02:49.480
If you're feeling uncomfortable about spin
at this point, feel free to watch that video

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.040
and ask me questions.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:57.140
Back to spin though: There is a pretty standard
way of measuring spin of a particle; it's

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.980
called the Stern- Gerlach machine.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:05.689
You set it up to measure spin along a certain
axis, say the y axis.

00:03:05.689 --> 00:03:08.669
Then what happens is electrons goes through
this passage.

00:03:08.669 --> 00:03:14.049
I won't go into how this works but if your
particle happened to be one that already had

00:03:14.049 --> 00:03:20.389
spin up in the y axis, it will get deflected
up and get measured around here.

00:03:20.389 --> 00:03:25.599
If the particle started with spin down in
y it will get deflected down.

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:28.409
How does Bohmian mechanics explain this?

00:03:28.409 --> 00:03:33.879
Remember that in Bohmian mechanics there is
that quantum force that depends on the wavefunction.

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:39.819
Well, spin is one of the things that influence
the wavefunction so the quantum force takes

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:45.639
spin into account, and then pushes the electrons
in the correct directions in these cases.

00:03:45.639 --> 00:03:51.049
Of course, most electrons have their spin
in other directions.

00:03:51.049 --> 00:03:56.040
What about if a bunch of electrons happened
to have their spin in the x direction which

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.470
is perpendicular to y?

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:03.620
As we saw in the spin video, when you measure
these particles in the y direction, half the

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:06.979
time the electron will have spin up and half
the time it will have spin down.

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:08.659
So in the experiment half these particles
travel up and half down.

00:04:08.659 --> 00:04:12.279
Again, Bohmian mechanics needs to explain
this.

00:04:12.279 --> 00:04:14.389
First we need to deal with something.

00:04:14.389 --> 00:04:20.269
There is no real randomness in Bohmian mechanics,
only randomness from ignorance, so we need

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:25.300
to be able to explain why half the particles
go one way and half the other way.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.090
This is how it's done.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:31.340
Let's say all the electrons start off somewhere
inside this little sphere, but we don't know

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.740
exactly where inside.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.770
If one of these particles starts in the top
half, you can calculate by Bohmian mechanics

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:43.889
that it will deflect up, and similarly for
the bottom half, giving exactly the right

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:45.020
result.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.900
No issues so far.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.419
Now let's look at sensibility condition number
1 again.

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:56.000
We measure that this particle that starts
here has spin up in the y direction, so our

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.470
criteria tells us, it must have had spin up
in that direction along.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:04.479
Again, that seems fairly innocuous.

00:05:04.479 --> 00:05:09.419
The problem is that there's another way to
measure the spin of that particular particle.

00:05:09.419 --> 00:05:15.930
Why not set the Stern-Gerlach machine to measure
spin in the negative y direction instead?

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:16.940
What should happen?

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:22.919
Well, we've already decided that this particle
has spin up in the y direction so by the properties

00:05:22.919 --> 00:05:26.740
of spin it must have spin down in the negative
y direction.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:30.879
Sorry, that's the last fact that I'll use
from that spin video.

00:05:30.879 --> 00:05:37.090
So that means that our particle is spin down
in the negative y direction so it's deflected

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:38.729
down now right?

00:05:38.729 --> 00:05:41.319
Nup, that's not what Bohmian mechanics says.

00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:48.430
It still goes up, which forces us to conclude
it has spin down in the y direction.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:53.110
And now we see how our 2nd sensibility criterion
is broken.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:58.030
If we measure the first way, our particle
will look like it had spin up, but if we measure

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:01.560
the second way, it will look like it had spin
down.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.729
Notice that I'm not saying Bohmian mechanics
makes the wrong experimental prediction, because

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:11.669
in the second case we also expect that half
the electrons deflect up and half deflect

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:14.830
down, and that's exactly what happens.

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:19.490
Bohmian mechanics can give you the right predictions
on any stern-gerlach experiment...

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:22.400
the issue is much more subtle than that.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.750
If we can't say our particle has property
X with out adding the proviso "so long as

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:33.280
we measure it this way", then what property
does the particle have before anyone decides

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:34.580
how to measure it?

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:39.970
I mean this seems like a quantum mechanics
level philosophical problem.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.870
So, should we scrap Bohmian mechanics and
look for some other, better alternative to

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.350
quantum?

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:47.350
Not so fast.

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:54.090
In fact, it's possible to prove that for any
theory that has sensibility criterion 1: That

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:59.590
experiments reveal preexisting properties,
then our second condition, that properties

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:05.440
don't depend on the type of experiment, will
always be broken- at least whenever you are

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.249
talking about spin.

00:07:07.249 --> 00:07:10.360
In other words, there are no completely sensible
theories

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:16.879
There's a lot of hype about this contextuality,
but I'm not sure how seriously we should take

00:07:16.879 --> 00:07:17.879
it.

00:07:17.879 --> 00:07:22.229
You see, this issue doesn't come up at all
when we're talking about most variables in

00:07:22.229 --> 00:07:27.090
Bohmian mechanics, like position, momentum
and energy.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:33.509
It's perfectly legitimate to say a particle
has this position, regardless of how you measure

00:07:33.509 --> 00:07:34.599
it.

00:07:34.599 --> 00:07:39.060
The problem only comes when you try and say
statements like that about spin and other

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.199
similar variables.

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:46.789
That's why some people say the theory isn't
broken, only the way we think about spin is.

00:07:46.789 --> 00:07:52.529
They say spin is not a real property of a
particle like position and momentum, instead

00:07:52.529 --> 00:07:58.499
it only comes into play in an indirect way:
the spin affects the wavefunctions which affects

00:07:58.499 --> 00:08:02.759
the quantum force which changes the direction
of the particle.

00:08:02.759 --> 00:08:08.969
It doesn't make sense to say "a particle has
spin up", instead, what you mean is the particle

00:08:08.969 --> 00:08:14.110
experiences some extra quantum force upward
because of spin.

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:19.029
I'm pretty conflicted about all this, and
I'd love hear your opinions about all this.

00:08:19.029 --> 00:08:25.210
Does Bohmian mechanics escape, or is this
really the final blow for all sensible alternatives

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:28.659
to quantum mechanics?

00:08:28.659 --> 00:08:34.529
I'd like to leave you with one deep thought
though: Spin... is weird.

